<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             xmlns:vm="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.ViewModels"
             mc:Ignorable="d" d:DesignWidth="900" d:DesignHeight="28"
             x:Class="KinesisEdit.Views.BoardLegendView"
             x:DataType="vm:BoardLegendViewModel">
  <!--
    The legend row under the keyboard canvas (mockups 1e/2a): a mono LEGEND label, then the five
    key states each drawn as its own badge beside a live count, then the row's two actions.

    THE SWATCHES ARE THE BADGE VOCABULARY, NOT DECORATION. Each one is the cap's own badge redrawn
    at legend size, out of the same token brush — the accent bar a remapped cap wears along its
    bottom edge, the macro dot, the tap-and-hold corner triangle, the amber advisory bar, the
    hatched face of a position that is not yours to edit. That is the whole point of the row: it
    teaches the board. If a badge's hue ever moves, it moves here too, because both name the token.

    SIZES ARE LEGEND SIZES, HUES ARE TOKENS. The cap's badges are 2-6px marks tuned to a 30x26 cap
    and would disappear in a text row, so the marks here are drawn a little larger; not one colour
    is a literal.

    THE COUNTS ARE SANS, THE LABEL IS MONO. "Mono is reserved for values that exist verbatim in the
    config file" — a count of remapped keys is the app talking, so it is sans; LEGEND is a section
    label, which the type scale defines as 10/600 uppercase mono.

    SCOPE: the Layout/Macros board only. The Lighting tab's picture draws no state badges
    (KeyboardView.ShowsStateBadges), so it counts no states either - the handoff's "every board view
    carries a legend row" is honoured where state is on screen. Recorded in docs/app/keyboard-editor.md.
  -->
  <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" VerticalAlignment="Center">

    <!-- The counts and the copy prompt share whatever width is left; the two actions never give
         ground. Column 0 is star and CLIPPED, which is load-bearing: a horizontal StackPanel
         measures its children at infinite width, so an unclipped Auto column does not shrink when
         the row runs out of room - it overflows to the right and paints over whatever is there.
         With the key inspector rail open at 1000x680 that was `Reset layer` sitting 4px under the
         rail, which every one of this row's assertions passed straight through (issue #92). The
         actions are what a user reaches for, so the counts are what gets cut. -->
    <Grid Grid.Column="0" ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*" ClipToBounds="True">

    <!-- ===== The counts ====================================================================== -->
    <!-- Space12, not Space16. With the inspector rail open at the 1000px floor the six items and
         the two actions want ~4px more than the row has, and the clip above would take that out of
         the last count's digit - a sliced `0` reads as a bug, where a tighter rhythm does not. -->
    <StackPanel Grid.Column="0" Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="12" VerticalAlignment="Center">
      <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:BoardLegendViewModel.LegendLabel}"
                 Classes="sectionLabel"
                 VerticalAlignment="Center" />

      <!-- Remapped: the 2px accent bar the cap carries across its bottom edge. Flat, not rounded —
           the advisory bar below is the rounded one, and the two must stay tellable apart. -->
      <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="6" VerticalAlignment="Center">
        <Border Width="14"
                Height="3"
                Background="{DynamicResource AccentBrush}"
                VerticalAlignment="Center" />
        <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:BoardLegendViewModel.RemappedCaption}"
                   Classes="meta muted"
                   VerticalAlignment="Center" />
        <TextBlock Text="{Binding RemappedCount}" Classes="meta" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
      </StackPanel>

      <!-- Macro: the cap's top-right dot. -->
      <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="6" VerticalAlignment="Center">
        <Ellipse Width="6"
                 Height="6"
                 Fill="{DynamicResource BadgeMacroBrush}"
                 VerticalAlignment="Center" />
        <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:BoardLegendViewModel.MacroCaption}"
                   Classes="meta muted"
                   VerticalAlignment="Center" />
        <TextBlock Text="{Binding MacroCount}" Classes="meta" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
      </StackPanel>

      <!-- Tap-and-hold: the cap's bottom-right corner triangle, drawn the same way round. -->
      <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="6" VerticalAlignment="Center">
        <Path Data="M 0,8 L 8,8 L 8,0 Z"
              Fill="{DynamicResource BadgeTapHoldBrush}"
              VerticalAlignment="Center" />
        <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:BoardLegendViewModel.TapAndHoldCaption}"
                   Classes="meta muted"
                   VerticalAlignment="Center" />
        <TextBlock Text="{Binding TapAndHoldCount}" Classes="meta" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
      </StackPanel>

      <!-- Advisory: the cap's 12x3 rounded amber bar, at exactly its own size. Amber, and only
           amber — an advisory is never red anywhere in this app. -->
      <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="6" VerticalAlignment="Center">
        <Border Width="{DynamicResource BadgeAdvisoryBarWidth}"
                Height="{DynamicResource BadgeAdvisoryBarHeight}"
                CornerRadius="{DynamicResource RadiusChip}"
                Background="{DynamicResource StatusAdvisoryStrongBrush}"
                VerticalAlignment="Center" />
        <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:BoardLegendViewModel.AdvisoryCaption}"
                   Classes="meta muted"
                   VerticalAlignment="Center" />
        <TextBlock Text="{Binding AdvisoryCount}" Classes="meta" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
      </StackPanel>

      <!-- Locked: a mini-cap wearing the hatch and the dashed border of a position that can never
           be remapped. A Rectangle rather than a Border because only a shape can carry a dash
           array, and the dash is half of what the design says "not yours to edit" with. -->
      <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="6" VerticalAlignment="Center">
        <Rectangle Width="12"
                   Height="10"
                   RadiusX="2"
                   RadiusY="2"
                   Fill="{DynamicResource HatchBrush}"
                   Stroke="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineHighBrush}"
                   StrokeThickness="1"
                   StrokeDashArray="2,2"
                   VerticalAlignment="Center" />
        <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:BoardLegendViewModel.LockedCaption}"
                   Classes="meta muted"
                   VerticalAlignment="Center" />
        <TextBlock Text="{Binding LockedCount}" Classes="meta" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
      </StackPanel>
    </StackPanel>

    <!-- ===== The copy prompt =================================================================
         What an armed `Copy key…` is waiting for, and what it says when the picked target refuses.

         ACCENT, NOT AMBER. The row is in an active editing mode, which is one of the accent's two
         sanctioned meanings; amber means "an advisory, which never blocks", and a pick that is
         waiting for a click is neither. `.badge` is the existing role for exactly this — a short
         accent tag beside the thing it qualifies. -->
    <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
               Text="{Binding CopyPrompt}"
               Classes="badge"
               Margin="16,0"
               TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
               VerticalAlignment="Center"
               IsVisible="{Binding IsPickingCopyTarget}" />

    </Grid>

    <!-- ===== The actions =====================================================================
         Both bordered neutrals, on the shared SecondaryButton role. `Reset layer` lives here, next
         to the counts it acts on, rather than in the provisional action row: sentence case, which
         is a deliberate deviation from spec 10's verbatim `Reset Layer` (docs/app/keyboard-editor.md).

         `Copy key…` is NOT the inspector's `Copy to…` of issue #92; it arms a pick on the board. -->
    <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8" VerticalAlignment="Center">
      <Button Classes="secondary"
              Content="{x:Static vm:BoardLegendViewModel.CopyKeyCaption}"
              Command="{Binding CopyKeyCommand}" />
      <Button Classes="secondary"
              Content="{x:Static vm:BoardLegendViewModel.ResetLayerCaption}"
              Command="{Binding ResetLayerCommand}" />
    </StackPanel>
  </Grid>
</UserControl>
